;;; `open` written as a fact's argument loads, as a name of the program —
;;; **W71**, register row `W71/fact-value`.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `(p open)` is a fact whose argument is `open`, a value.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `open` is the verdict a rule's `:assert` states, `(open)` or `(open ?R)`,
;;; and is written nowhere else (Q-M1f.66):
;;; here it is written as a fact's argument, so the program is refused at the
;;; parse, at the `open`, and every command exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `f62e34e1`, release: the program loads, and `open` is a name of the
;;; program, so the blind enumerator proposes `(a open)` and `(q open)`: R is 16
;;; states, its maximal `(model (a k) (a open) (p k) (p open) (q k) (q open))`,
;;; exit 0 on every command.
;;; The engine refuses it at the parse.
;;;

(relation a Thing :why "{?1} is an a")
(relation p Thing :why "{?1} is a p")
(relation q Thing :why "{?1} is a q")
(is-a k Thing)
(a k)
(p open)
